     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the white powder on my rosemary leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A white, powdery coating on rosemary leaves is powdery mildew, a fungal disease. NC State Extension says poor air circulation and high humidity cause it on rosemary, and UC IPM says shade and moderate temperatures favour it, while heat above about 35 °C (95 °F) can hold it back. Crowded plants and plants in shade or still air are most at risk."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my rosemary drying out and turning crispy in summer?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Crispy rosemary in a UAE summer usually means the roots dried out in a small or hot pot, or the plant took full afternoon sun and hot wind. The University of Arizona Extension warns that pots in direct summer sun heat the roots and small pots are very hard to keep moist."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my indoor rosemary thin and spindly?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Thin, spindly rosemary indoors is not getting enough light. Rosemary needs six or more hours of direct sun a day, which a room away from a sunny window cannot give. Penn State Extension says herbs without the light they need grow thin and spindly, with smaller leaves."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the tiny insects on my rosemary?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Rosemary is generally free of pests, but NC State Extension says to watch for aphids, mealybugs, whiteflies and spider mites, especially on plants kept indoors. UC IPM says fine webbing and leaves stippled with pale dots point to spider mites (tiny relatives of spiders), small white insects that fly up when disturbed are whiteflies, and white, waxy fluff is mealybugs."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my rosemary woody and bare at the bottom?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Rosemary turns woody and bare at the base when it is not trimmed. The RHS says unpruned rosemary becomes leggy, that you should not cut back into old wood, and that old, straggly plants are best replaced with new plants grown from cuttings."
     }
    },
